WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 4 Student Video
Unit 1, Lesson 4: Logic

1
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.380
When you think of logic, who comes to mind?

2
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.300
Probably somebody like Sherlock Holmes, who uses his wit and mind to

3
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.040
outsmart his opponents.

4
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.040
Maybe you feel this way when you've had a conversation with somebody with a

5
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.520
different belief system or when you're sharing your faith.

6
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.760
It's as if it's this jockeying back and forth trying to outsmart each other with

7
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:42.360
logic. Well, this can certainly happen, but technically logic is just a careful and

8
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.120
proper use of reason. Logic is important for Christians so we can arrive at

9
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.860
truthful beliefs ourselves, but it's also important in conversations with other

10
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.780
people so we can help them arrive at truth in a loving manner.

11
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.640
Let me share a story that might help.

12
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.660
some time ago when I was a graduate student studying philosophy I had a memorable

13
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:12.060
conversation with a professor from an Ivy League school he said to me he goes oh I

14
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.660
think it's great that you're studying philosophy as a Christian maybe you'd want to

15
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:21.520
study Buddhist philosophy because all religions are true now that struck me as very

16
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.700
odd I thought for a minute wait a minute how could all religions be true when they

17
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.720
contradict on such fundamental teachings about God, the afterlife, and how you get

18
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.060
salvation. So I simply asked him a question.

19
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.920
I said, do you mind if I ask you a question?

20
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.540
How could all religions be true, namely Islam and Christianity?

21
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.040
Muslims say if you believe Jesus is God, you've committed the sin of shirk, and

22
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.980
you're certainly going to hell. I said, Christians say you can't get to heaven

23
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.740
unless you believe Jesus is God.

24
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.680
How can both of these be true? He kind of fumbled around a little bit.

25
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.060
We kept talking. Eventually he looked at me and said, here's the deal.

26
00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.740
He said, religion aside, what really is true is cultural relativism.

27
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.220
Therefore, you should be tolerant.

28
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:11.980
Now stop and think about this. Use your mind, your logic to analyze this claim.

29
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.760
He said he believed in cultural relativism, which says morality and truth depends

30
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.120
upon the culture. But then he turned to me and said, therefore, because

31
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.460
morality is relative, I should be tolerant.

32
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.660
Now do you see the inconsistency?

33
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.740
If morality is relative, he can't turn to me and say I ought to be tolerant because

34
00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.180
he's appealing to tolerance as if it's a universal truth claim that everybody should

35
00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:46.040
follow. On the flip side, if he says that everybody should be tolerant, then he

36
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.580
doesn't actually believe that morality is relative because he's appealing to the

37
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.860
universal principle or standard of tolerance.

38
00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.200
Friends, people hold a lot of different views about God, religion and morality and

39
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.100
one important thing to do is to listen to their views and draw it out and what we'll

40
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.340
often see is when we do that their views will lead to inconsistency and sometimes

41
00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.560
even to logical absurdity.

42
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:16.240
For example in Matthew 22:41 through 45 Jesus uses the known

43
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.440
logical principle of reductio ad absurdum.

44
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.320
Now that sounds really fancy, but essentially what it means is reducing a position

45
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.960
to absurdity. In this passage in Matthew, Jesus is having a conversation with the

46
00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.480
Pharisees and he asks them, who do you think the Messiah is?

47
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.740
Is the Messiah the son of David?

48
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.340
And they say yes, the Messiah is the son of David.

49
00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:45.100
And what Jesus says is meant to draw out their view to show that it leads to

50
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.840
absurdity. Now, how does he do this?

51
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:52.560
He quotes from Psalms 110:1 in which David, who wrote that psalm, says

52
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.780
that the Messiah is his Lord. And Jesus says, wait a minute, you just said that the

53
00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.180
Messiah is the son of David, but now we have David calling the Messiah Lord.

54
00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.820
How can that be? Now, what is Jesus' point?

55
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.860
He's saying if you think that the Messiah is merely the son of David, then that

56
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.600
leads to absurdity. But rather it's more reasonable to conclude that yes, the

57
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.220
Messiah is the son of David but he's more than human.

58
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.300
He actually is Lord as David himself said in Psalms 110 verse 1.

59
00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.460
In my conversation with an Ivy League professor I was trying to show that he held

60
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:35.120
two views in contradiction that when drawn to their logical conclusion led to

61
00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.700
absurdity. This is exactly what Jesus was doing with the Pharisees.

62
00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:45.940
This is why the Apostle Paul in Ephesians 4 verses 14 through 15 says we are not

63
00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:51.520
to be like children in terms of our thinking, tossed to and fro by different waves

64
00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.600
of doctrine. Rather, we are to grow up and be mature in the way we think.

65
00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.040
Paul also says in this passage that we are to speak truth in love.

66
00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.080
In other words, we are called to speak truth.

67
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.320
We can't compromise truth. We don't speak truth just to win arguments.

68
00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.080
We don't speak truth just to prove people wrong.

69
00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:18.340
We do it because we know that it's only truth that can set people free.

70
00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.540
And please keep in mind, your conversations will not always go as smoothly as they

71
00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.480
seem to go with me and the Ivy League professor.

72
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.420
In fact, more often than not, after a conversation I have with somebody,

73
00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.900
I'll step away and go, oh, I should've said this.

74
00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.520
Oh, next time, I'll say that. Learning to love God with our minds and use logic is a

75
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:44.480
life-long process. You're learning some powerful stuff about truth and defending

76
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.060
the faith in this entire study that we're going through.

77
00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.420
It's easy to start thinking, great, I'm gonna write this down, I'm gonna go online,

78
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.500
I'm gonna go in person to my parents, my friends, other nonbelievers, and win some

79
00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.440
arguments. if that's what you're thinking you're missing the point the point of this

80
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.920
is not to go out and win arguments the point of this is number one to know truth for

81
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.480
ourselves and then second to be able to speak that truth in love to use

82
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:16.060
reason to use logic to love people that way as people draw closer to you

83
00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:21.040
learn more about your faith they find something genuine and real that is not easily

84
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:21.980
put out

